{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And he commanded them, saying.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say <b>לֵאמֹר</b> after he already said ״כֹּה תֹאמְרוּן לְעֵשָׂו״. And perhaps it intends to say that they should tell him that he commanded them to say to him in this order, for otherwise he would think that the emissaries themselves are the ones speaking in this formulation, considering him the firstborn in his firstborn status — and it is proper etiquette to speak thus — and that these words did not come from the mouth of Jacob. Therefore he commanded them to say that it was he who spoke these words, and to call him 'master' over him, for by this he would remove jealousy and hatred. And with this my mind is satisfied as to why Jacob refers to Esav by the name 'master' even not in his presence — which might magnify over him the portion of evil, Heaven forbid — and according to what I have written, these are words said to Esav to flatter the wicked man so that he would not enter into war with him." "<b>With Laban I sojourned, etc.</b> One needs to know what he informs by this, and also what he informs in the entire message. And perhaps it is because the way of two brothers who are faithful in their love for each other is for one brother to inform the other of everything that befalls him, both the good and the bad, for this demonstrates the closeness of their hearts. And therefore Jacob recounted all his experiences, good and bad, to find favor in his eyes when he knows that he trusts him and that his brotherhood is faithful." "<b>Further,</b> he was cunning in this message to inform him that he is not worthy of being distanced — and go out and learn from <b>עִם לָבָן גַּרְתִּי</b>: Laban was well known for his extreme wickedness and deceitfulness, and no man dwells with a snake; and even so, Jacob sojourned with him. And this is for you a sign of the excellence of his upright deeds, and it is fitting and proper to draw him near and to love him and to cherish him. And do not say that this was for a brief time — rather <b>וָאֵחַר עַד עָתָּה</b>; and do not say that it was insignificant — a servant to him who was not worth the groaning of his belly — therefore he said <b>וַיְהִי לִי שׁוֹר</b> etc., and even so, I am preparing myself to be a servant to you and my possessions are your possessions, and these words will melt a heart of stone."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:35.041181187Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}